00:00:01.579 --> 00:00:03.710 align:start position:0%
the centerpiece of relations between the  European Union and the United States is  the transatlantic trade and investment  partnership or TTIP said to be the  biggest bilateral trade pact in the  world the Accord launched a year ago and  designed to drive growth and jobs could  boost the EU economy by 120 billion  euros annually and the US economy by 90  billion euros according to some  estimates but its conclusion hopefully  in the next few years would also give  impetus to world trade talks which have  been at a virtual standstill for several  years the aim of the T tip is to remove  trade barriers in a wide range of  economic sectors to make it easier to  buy and sell goods and services between  the EU and the United States  transatlantic trade totals around 2  billion euros each day and every barrier  lifted could mean big savings the  negotiations focus on three broad themes  market access regulatory issues and  non-tariff barriers and the rules under  which the trade pact would operate on  top of cutting tariffs the EU and the  u.s. want to tackle barriers behind the  customs border like differences in  technical regulations standards and  approval procedures which cost time and  money for example when a car is approved  as safe in the EU it has to undergo a  new approval procedure in the u.s. even  though the safety standards are similar  the TTIP negotiations will also look at  opening markets in services investment  and public procurement
 

